{
  "abstract": "For a nonempty finite set of integers excluding 0, 1, and −1, consider the primes for which every integer in the set is a primitive root. When neither Matthews obstruction occurs, the simultaneous primitive primes have a lower bound of order x/log x in every sufficiently large real dyadic interval (x, 2x] and positive lower relative density among the primes. When either obstruction occurs, the simultaneous primitive primes form a finite set. This is Theorem 1.1 of the accompanying paper.",
